---
<ArticleLayout
  title="How Often Should You Change a Furnace Filter?"
  description="Learn how often to inspect and change a furnace filter, what makes filters load faster, and how to record the correct size and airflow direction."
  canonicalPath={article.path}
  h1="How Often Should You Change a Furnace Filter?"
  standfirst="Inspect it monthly, but let the filter type, equipment instructions, operating conditions, and what you actually see determine when it should be cleaned or replaced."
  published="2026-09-09"
  updated="2026-09-09"
  breadcrumbLabel={article.label}
>
  <h2>Use a monthly check, not a universal replacement date</h2>
  <p>ENERGY STAR recommends checking a central air conditioner, furnace, or heat-pump filter every month and cleaning or changing it when dirty. It also gives three months as a minimum replacement interval for many disposable filters, while Carrier notes that common 1-inch filters may need replacement from every 30 days to three months and thicker 4-inch filters may last three to six months.</p>
  <p>Those ranges are starting points—not permission to override your equipment or filter instructions. Some systems use washable media, specialty cabinets, or controls with their own schedule.</p>

  <h2>Check more often when the filter loads faster</h2>
  <p>Heavy heating or cooling use, renovation dust, smoke, pets, open windows, and a dusty environment can shorten the useful interval. Inspect sooner after a change in household conditions. A visibly dirty filter, increased dust, weaker airflow, or longer system cycles deserves attention.</p>

  <h2>How to replace a typical disposable filter</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Check the furnace or air-handler manual for the filter location, size, type, and permitted efficiency rating.</li>
    <li>Turn the system off using the procedure in the manual.</li>
    <li>Before removing the filter, photograph its dimensions and airflow arrow.</li>
    <li>Slide it out carefully so accumulated dust does not spill into the cabinet.</li>
    <li>Install the correct replacement with its airflow arrow pointing the same direction as the old filter—generally toward the blower.</li>
    <li>Close the access panel fully, restore operation, and confirm normal airflow.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Do not substitute a higher-resistance filter merely because it has a higher rating. Use only a filter the equipment manufacturer permits. Call an HVAC professional if the filter is missing, does not fit, collapses, becomes wet, or dirt is bypassing it.</p>

  <h2>Save the details that prevent the next mistake</h2>
  <div class="article-example">
    <dl>
      <dt>Item</dt><dd>Main HVAC return filter</dd>
      <dt>Record</dt><dd>Actual dimensions, part number, permitted rating, airflow direction</dd>
      <dt>Completion</dt><dd>Date changed and condition of the removed filter</dd>
      <dt>Next check</dt><dd>One month, adjusted after observing how quickly it loads</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

